---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";

/**
 * The language page. Sourced from `mzizi-dev/mzizi/CHARTER.md` (§3 design
 * goals, §4 phasing, §5 non-goals, §6 the resolved benchmark design) and the
 * four RFCs in that repository's `design/` directory. Where the charter leaves
 * something open, this page says so rather than rounding it up.
 */

const goals = [
  {
    name: "Low syntactic ambiguity",
    why: "Every degree of freedom in how a thing could have been written is a degree of freedom a model can get subtly wrong. Fewer distinct-but-equivalent ways to express one intent.",
  },
  {
    name: "Dense, high-signal errors",
    why: "The agentic loop is generate → compile → read error → fix. Its quality is bounded by how much actionable information the compiler packs into each character of output. This is compiler UX aimed at a machine reader.",
  },
  {
    name: "Fast incremental compilation",
    why: "A human tolerates a few seconds per iteration. An agent iterating hundreds of times per session treats compile latency as the dominant cost of the whole workflow — so it is a Phase 0 success metric, not a later optimisation.",
  },
  {
    name: "Token-efficient representation",
    why: "A codebase that fits more real logic into a context window per token spent is one an agent can reason about with less summarisation loss. It applies to the syntax surface and to the IR the tooling exposes.",
  },
];

const phases = [
  {
    n: "Phase 0",
    title: "Prove the core claim, no rendering attached",
    state: "in progress, unmeasured",
    body: "A standalone compiler and syntax prototype with zero UI story. Success is a defined benchmark: an agent authors equivalent components in Mzizi syntax versus raw Dioxus or Leptos, scored on tokens consumed, iterations to a clean compile, and defect rate. If that shows no measurable advantage, nothing downstream matters.",
  },
  {
    n: "Phase 1",
    title: "Rendering interop; WASM and native as the deliverable",
    state: "not started",
    body: "Wire the compiler output into Dioxus's existing renderer. The artifact — a WASM bundle, and a native binary for desktop — is the cross-platform product, embeddable from a bare script tag, a mobile WebView, or an edge runtime.",
  },
  {
    n: "Phase 2",
    title: "Edge-first deployment",
    state: "not started",
    body: "A native target built on workers-rs. The cheapest phase to execute, because it is the one the team has already shipped against.",
  },
  {
    n: "Phase 3",
    title: "Candle integration",
    state: "not started",
    body: "ML inference declared as a first-class capability inside a component, backed by Candle. Not a competing tensor runtime.",
  },
  {
    n: "Phase 4",
    title: "Distribution adapters",
    state: "not started",
    body: "Thin packaging layers over the Phase 1 artifact — Astro first, because it is furthest along, not because Astro is a platform Mzizi targets.",
  },
];

const rfcs = [
  {
    id: "RFC-0001",
    file: "RFC-0001-syntax.md",
    what: "The nine failure modes an agent hits writing Rust UI code, and the syntax that answers each: end <kind> <name>, one construct per intent, enum data columns, contracts, capabilities, no ownership surface.",
  },
  {
    id: "RFC-0002",
    file: "RFC-0002-runtime-and-prior-art.md",
    what: "The design-target correction — small open-weight models, not frontier ones — and why the runtime is the product. Thirteen languages mined for ideas, with the licence discipline that keeps the tree clean.",
  },
  {
    id: "RFC-0003",
    file: "RFC-0003-ir.md",
    what: "The eight barriers an agent hits reading a codebase, and the content-addressed IR that answers them: incremental compiles, semantic patching, caching and free renames from one decision.",
  },
  {
    id: "RFC-0004",
    file: "RFC-0004-test-topology.md",
    what: "What is public (nearly all the testing) and what is held out (the benchmark task set), why, and the rule that keeps forks working: private consumes public, never the reverse.",
  },
];
---

<Site
  title="The language"
  description="Mzizi-lang: a Rust-lowering language whose syntax, type system and compiler feedback loop are designed for machine authorship, and specifically for small open-weight models."
  lede="Designed for machine authorship — and specifically for the machines that need the help most: small open-weight models with limited parameters, context and long-range attention."
>
  <p>
    Mzizi lowers to Rust and Dioxus. The novel part, and the only part worth
    defending as research, is the syntax, the type system and the compiler's
    feedback loop. Cross-platform rendering is interop with Dioxus rather than a
    rebuilt engine; ML is Candle; the edge target is <code>workers-rs</code>.
    Reinventing any of those would buy the compiler layer nothing and would cost
    Phase 0 its focus.
  </p>

  <div class="status">
    <h2>What exists, and what that does not prove</h2>
    <p>
      Real today: the lexer, a recovering parser, the agent diagnostic protocol,
      the content-addressed IR, and nine primitives written in Mzizi itself,
      covered by 75 tests. Contract blocks parse but are not yet evaluated,
      which is why the defect metric below is still a hand-written Rust test
      rather than something the toolchain measures.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The Phase 0 benchmark has not run.</strong> Nothing on this page has
      been measured against the charter's kill criteria. The four design goals below
      are hypotheses with arguments attached — that is a real and useful thing to
      publish, and it is not the same thing as a result.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What "built for machine authorship" cashes out as</h2>

  <p>
    The charter insists this be measurable design goals rather than a slogan.
    There are four.
  </p>

  <div class="scroller">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Goal</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it is on the list</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          goals.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{g.name}</th>
              <td>{g.why}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How the benchmark is defined</h2>

  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      <strong>Task set:</strong> Mzizi's own components — the design system already
      built out in the registry — authored in Mzizi syntax against the existing implementations
      as ground truth. Explicitly not a port of an external library.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Defect:</strong> code that compiles cleanly but is behaviourally wrong
      — it passes the compiler and fails a contract test against the reference. A
      syntax or compile error is not a defect for this metric; it is the ordinary
      friction the error-density goal exists to reduce. The metric measures what gets
      <em>past</em> the compiler wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Visibility:</strong> the harness is public — runner, metric definitions,
      scoring code, fixture format. The held-out task set and its expected outputs
      are not, and live in a separate private repository. That is a measurement-validity
      requirement rather than a secrecy preference: a public task set gets scraped
      into training data, after which the benchmark measures memorisation, a contaminated
      benchmark looks like a successful one, and the kill criterion could never fire.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Phasing</h2>

  <p>
    Research portfolios ship one thread at a time or ship nothing. This is the
    order, not a wishlist.
  </p>

  <div class="scroller">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Phase</th>
          <th scope="col">State</th>
          <th scope="col">What it is</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          phases.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                {p.n}
                <br />
                <span class="state">{p.title}</span>
              </th>
              <td class="state">{p.state}</td>
              <td>{p.body}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Stated non-goals</h2>

  <ul class="spaced">
    <li>Not a competing tensor or ML runtime. Candle is the dependency.</li>
    <li>
      Not a native cross-platform renderer in Phase 0 or 1. Dioxus interop
      first; a native renderer only if interop is proven insufficient.
    </li>
    <li>
      Not post-quantum cryptography. Named as a future research thread and
      deliberately kept out of this charter's scope.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The RFCs</h2>

  <p>
    These are the design record, and they are the thing to read before the code.
  </p>

  <div class="scroller">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">RFC</th>
          <th scope="col">What it settles</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rfcs.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a
                  href={`https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi/blob/main/design/${r.file}`}
                >
                  {r.id}
                </a>
              </th>
              <td>{r.what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Trying it</h2>

  <p>
    The compiler is the <code>mz</code> binary in
    <a href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi">mzizi-dev/mzizi</a>. The surface
    an agent should use is <code>mz check --agent</code>: whole-program NDJSON in
    a deterministic order, at most one diagnostic per real error, every message written
    for a reader with no prior context, and machine-applicable fixes tagged
    <code>exact</code> or <code>guess</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start with <a href="https://github.com/mzizi-dev/mzizi/blob/main/CHARTER.md"
      >CHARTER.md</a
    >, which carries the full thesis, the phasing, the resolved benchmark
    decisions and the questions still open.
  </p>
</Site>
